@@ -0,0 +1,94 @@
+# SOLUTION
+
+`make test` was failing due to four independent environment/config problems left
+behind by the botched merge. No Python source logic was changed, and no check was
+removed or weakened.
+
+Each problem masked the next, so they surfaced one at a time.
+
+## 1. `Makefile` — recipe indented with spaces instead of a tab
+
+**Symptom:** `Makefile:4: *** missing separator.  Stop.`
+
+Line 4 (the `python3 -m pytest -q tests/` recipe under `test:`) was indented with
+four spaces. Make requires a literal tab to introduce a recipe line. The `check:`
+recipe on line 7 still had its tab, so only the `test` recipe was affected —
+typical of a merge tool normalizing whitespace.
+
+**Fix:** Replaced the four leading spaces on line 4 with a tab.
+
+## 2. `scripts/run_checks.sh` — lost its executable bit
+
+**Symptom:** `make: ./scripts/run_checks.sh: Permission denied` → `Error 127`
+
+The file was mode `644`, but the `check` target invokes it directly as
+`./scripts/run_checks.sh`. Git had it recorded as `100644`, so the exec bit was
+lost in the merge rather than only locally.
+
+**Fix:** `chmod +x scripts/run_checks.sh` (now `100755`). Left the Makefile
+invoking the script exactly as before rather than working around it with
+`bash scripts/...`.
+
+## 3. `scripts/run_checks.sh` — CRLF line endings
+
+**Symptom:** `make: ./scripts/run_checks.sh: No such file or directory` — a
+misleading error, since the script plainly existed and was now executable.
+
+The file had Windows CRLF terminators, so the shebang read as `#!/bin/bash\r`.
+The kernel searched for an interpreter literally named `/bin/bash\r`, which does
+not exist, and reported the *interpreter* as missing. This file was the only one
+in the repo with CRLF endings.
+
+**Fix:** Rewrote the file with LF endings; contents are otherwise byte-identical
+(the diff shows all 4 lines changed purely because of the terminators).
+
+Note: there is no `.gitattributes` and `core.autocrlf` is unset, so the CRLF came
+in with the merge and will not be reintroduced on checkout.
+
+## 4. `data/config.json` — trailing comma made it invalid JSON
+
+**Symptom:** `json.decoder.JSONDecodeError: Expecting property name enclosed in
+double quotes: line 5 column 1` raised by `tools/validate_config.py`.
+
+`"batch_size": 500,` had a trailing comma before the closing brace. JSON does not
+permit trailing commas, so `json.load` failed before validation could run.
+
+**Fix:** Removed the trailing comma. All config *values* are unchanged; the
+validator itself was not touched.
+
+## Verification
+
+`make test` exits 0, running both the check step and the full test suite:
+
+```
+./scripts/run_checks.sh
+config ok: data/config.json
+all checks passed
+python3 -m pytest -q tests/
+...                                                                      [100%]
+3 passed in 0.01s
+```
+
+To confirm the config check is still genuinely gating the suite (i.e. that
+problem 4 was fixed rather than bypassed), `make test` was re-run against three
+deliberately broken configs. All three still fail the build:
+
+| Injected fault | `make test` exit |
+| --- | --- |
+| trailing comma / invalid JSON | non-zero |
+| missing required key `destination` | non-zero |
+| `batch_size: 0` | non-zero |
+
+The config was restored afterwards and the final run passes.
+
+## Files changed
+
+```
+ Makefile              | 2 +-   (spaces -> tab)
+ data/config.json      | 2 +-   (trailing comma removed)
+ scripts/run_checks.sh | 8 +-   (CRLF -> LF; contents identical)
+ mode change 100644 => 100755 scripts/run_checks.sh
+```
+
+Nothing under `tests/` was modified, and `tools/validate_config.py`,
+`src/pipeline/*`, and `conftest.py` are untouched. No git commits were created.
